Here's how to unit test a Spring Boot controller endpoint properly — isolating the web layer from the service/repository layers.

Approach: @WebMvcTest + MockMvc
This loads only the web layer (controller, filters, @ControllerAdvice, Jackson config) instead of the full application context.

java
@WebMvcTest(UserController.class)
class UserControllerTest {

    @Autowired
    private MockMvc mockMvc;

    @MockBean
    private UserService userService; // mock out the service layer

    @Test
    void getUser_returnsUserJson_whenUserExists() throws Exception {
        User user = new User(1L, "Leonel", "leonel@example.com");
        when(userService.findById(1L)).thenReturn(user);

        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/users/{id}", 1L)
                .accept(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON))
            .andExpect(status().isOk())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.id").value(1L))
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.name").value("Leonel"));
    }

    @Test
    void getUser_returns404_whenUserNotFound() throws Exception {
        when(userService.findById(99L))
            .thenThrow(new UserNotFoundException(99L));

        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/users/{id}", 99L))
            .andExpect(status().isNotFound());
    }

    @Test
    void createUser_returns201_withLocationHeader() throws Exception {
        String body = """
            {"name": "Leonel", "email": "leonel@example.com"}
            """;

        when(userService.create(any())).thenReturn(new User(5L, "Leonel", "leonel@example.com"));

        mockMvc.perform(post("/api/users")
                .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
                .content(body))
            .andExpect(status().isCreated())
            .andExpect(header().string("Location", "/api/users/5"));
    }
}
Key points:

@MockBean (or @MockitoBean if you're on Spring Boot 3.4+, since @MockBean is deprecated there) replaces the real service bean in the context, so nothing hits the DB or downstream calls — a true unit test of the controller's mapping/serialization/validation logic.
jsonPath lets you assert on the response body without deserializing manually.
Test the exception → HTTP status mapping if you have a @ControllerAdvice/@ExceptionHandler — @WebMvcTest loads those too, so you can verify your error handling actually produces the right status/body.
For validation (@Valid on request DTOs), send an invalid payload and assert status().isBadRequest().
Alternative: standalone MockMvcBuilders
If you don't want to spin up any Spring context at all (faster, more "pure" unit test):

java
@BeforeEach
void setup() {
    UserService mockService = mock(UserService.class);
    UserController controller = new UserController(mockService);
    mockMvc = MockMvcBuilders.standaloneSetup(controller)
        .setControllerAdvice(new GlobalExceptionHandler())
        .build();
}
This skips Spring's auto-config entirely, so it's faster but you lose things like your real @ControllerAdvice wiring unless you register it manually, and any custom HandlerMethodArgumentResolvers or converters you rely on in production.

Which to use: @WebMvcTest is generally the better default — it exercises the actual Spring MVC wiring (message converters, validation, exception handling) closer to production behavior, at a small startup cost per test class. Reach for standalone setup only when you want zero Spring context overhead and are testing pure controller logic in isolation.

If you're testing security-annotated endpoints (@PreAuthorize, etc.), you'll also want spring-security-test and @WithMockUser — let me know if that's part of what you're testing and I can add that.






